Here I forgot that if you do get a wish and the throne doesn't
disappear, you are free to try again. If we take this into account we get:
                            sit on the throne
                           /                 \
                          /                   \ 1/3
                     2/3 /                     \
                        /                       \
                    nothing                    something
                    /     \                   /   |     \
               1/3 /       \ 2/3             /    | 1/13 \ 1/13
                 GONE     REPEAT            /   REPEAT   !tele
                                           /             /    \
                                     1/13 /         1/3 /      \ 2/3
                                         /            GONE    REPEAT
                                     got_wish
                                    /        \
                               1/3 /          \ 2/3

                                 GONE      [second_sit]

Where [second sit] looks exactly like the the above tree, except that,
in it, "[second_sit]" is changed to "END" and thus the recursion terminates.

Then we have:

Pr[2 consecutive wishes at once] =
1/3 * 1/13 * 2/3 * 1/3 * 1/3 = 2/4563

let keep = 2/3 * 2/3 + 1/3 * 1/13 + 1/3 * 11/13 * 2/3
// keep probability from Eric Hesselink's original tree, i.e. from those
// branches that don't extend into the recursion.

Pr[keep throne] =
keep + keep * (1/3 * 1/13 * 2/3)
// account also for those that do extend (with the prefix (1/3 * 1/13 //
// * 2/3)

So it would be:
Pr[2 consecutive wishes] =

                                   inf
Pr[2 consecutive wishes at once] * Sum (Pr[keep throne])^n
                                   n=0

= (if my calclulations is correct)

         inf
2/4563 * Sum (9169/13689)
         n=0

= 3/1130.
